### Deletion of the Kth Element of a Linked List

**Problem:** Given the head of a singly linked list and an integer `k`, delete the Kth node from the linked list and return the head of the modified linked list.

**Approach:**

- Check if the linked list is empty (`head == null`). If so, return `null`.
- If `k == 1`, delete the head node by updating `head = head.next`.
- Otherwise, initialize a temporary pointer with the head node.
- Traverse the linked list until reaching the `(k-1)th` node.
- Update the next reference of the previous node to skip the Kth node (`temp.next = temp.next.next`).
- This removes the Kth node from the linked list.
- Return the updated head.

**Time Complexity:** O(n) – In the worst case, we traverse the linked list to reach the Kth node.

**Space Complexity:** O(1) – Only a temporary pointer is used.

**Concepts Used:**
- Singly Linked List
- Kth Node Deletion
- Head Pointer
- Temporary Pointer
- Pointer Manipulation
- Node Linking
- Null Checking


###Solution

class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteKthNode(ListNode head, int k) {
        // Case 1: If the list is empty
        if (head == null) {
            return null;
        }

        // Case 2: Deleting the 1st node (head)
        if (k == 1) {
            return head.next;
        }

        // Case 3: Deleting any node after the 1st
        ListNode current = head;
        int count = 1;

        // Traverse to the (k - 1)th node
        while (current != null && count < k - 1) {
            current = current.next;
            count++;
        }

        // If the kth node exists, bypass it
        if (current != null && current.next != null) {
            current.next = current.next.next;
        }

        return head;
    }
}
